The Python pilot proved the fact could be recorded; this makes it usable.
fastapi.yaml and flask.yaml now tag `app = FastAPI()` / `Flask(__name__)`
as concept: application and APIRouter()/Blueprint() as concept: router —
the first framework integrations written against a CONSTRUCTION site.
The contrast is the point: aiohttp.yaml reaches its Application with a
plain base_class rule because you subclass it; FastAPI's is constructed,
and until now no rule could reach it at all. docs/CONCEPTS.md regenerated:
fastapi and flask now appear under both concepts.

A PARITY COLUMN FIRST, AND IT CHANGED THE PLAN. "Eight analyzers need the
key" was the scope until test_constructed_from_parity measured it. There
are TWO hole shapes: six languages were KEY MISSING (the analyzer emits
the binding and just doesn't stamp the callee — javascript, typescript,
go, swift, rust, clojure, all now closed) and three are NO SYMBOL (ruby,
php, elixir emit no module-level variable at all, so there is nothing to
stamp). The second is a prerequisite gap of a different size, filed
separately rather than bundled. Holes are strict xfails carrying which
shape they are.

ONE SHARED HELPER, NOT SIX COPIES. analyze.base.constructed_from_callee
owns the rule and its three narrowings for every tree-sitter analyzer;
Clojure's s-expression form is the one genuine exception. The helper
falls back to a leading name-shaped child when a grammar names no callee
field — which is how Swift works without a special case, since its
call_expression is simply (simple_identifier, call_suffix) with no fields
at all. Go needed its own unwrap: the `value` field resolves to an
expression_list rather than being absent, and assuming absence is why Go
silently recorded nothing on the first attempt.

The expected callee is PER-LANGUAGE. Rust's idiom is an associated
function (C::new), Go's a constructor function (NewC), JS uses new C().
A single shared expectation quietly mis-passed Rust until it was split.

CAUGHT BY MEASURING END-TO-END, NOT BY REVIEW: the first version of the
YAML patterns was appended to the end of fastapi.yaml, which put them
inside the `linkers:` list rather than `patterns:`. yamllint passed, the
file parsed, and load_framework_patterns returned ZERO of them. A
regression test now asserts the loader sees them.

Swift's coverage exposed a new smart-test selection route (recorded on
INV-kinin): BRANCHES_test_swift.py drives the analyzer through the CLI,
so the import-graph selector links it to cli.py and not to swift.py — it
was the sole coverer of the protocol-requirement branch and was absent
from the manifest. Worked around with a direct-import test in
test_swift.py; the class stays open.

New item: WI-kavam (ruby/php/elixir emit no module-level variable).

